  "worked_solution": "We proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we split each whole into thirds.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/d9c692ed44d98c47b475dc18c2d28a01d0ab1f2f231ed24a9604f14000f599b1.png)\n\n \n\n- Next, we create groups of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/3c9fd6222f68deffa02ce69a2f31dca220ce2285f71dbd8255d364126ea0d8c8.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, we count the groups.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/228c9e5eea2b5696d64d4423ecd927e12a11e6672d614cc30a947549943be0c4.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $2$ wholes into groups of $\\frac{2}{3},$ we get $3$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2÷\\frac{2}{3}=\\boxed{\\text{3}}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $3.$",
